# Project Rules — WebRTC Room

## Language & Edition
- Rust edition 2024 (requires Rust 1.85+)
- No `unsafe` unless justified with a `// SAFETY:` comment
- Prefer `thiserror` for error enums, `anyhow` only in `main`/binaries

## Frontend
- Leptos 0.8+ (CSR mode — WebRTC is inherently client-side)
- Semantic HTML only (`<main>`, `<section>`, `<header>`, `<figure>`, `<video>`, `<details>`, etc.)
- CSS: PicoCSS 2.x, **vendored as Sass sources under `style/pico/`** — no CDN, no third-party request from the page
- Styling: SCSS in one entry file (`style/main.scss`), compiled by cargo-leptos' Dart Sass; no CSS-in-JS, no Tailwind; custom rules use `var(--pico-*)` hooks
- Icons: none for MVP (future: inline SVG spritesheet)
- All user-facing strings: inline for now (future: `rust-i18n` or similar)

## Signaling
- **SSE (Server-Sent Events)** for server → client push — NOT WebSocket
- Scope of that rule: application traffic only. `cargo leptos watch` injects a
  browser-refresh channel served by the build tool on loopback `:3001`; it is
  emitted only when `LEPTOS_WATCH` is set, is absent from production output, and
  has no SSE variant. It is not application push and is exempt.
- Regular `POST /signal` for client → server (SDP offers/answers, ICE candidates)
- **Media signals are addressed**: every offer/answer/ICE carries `to` (a `session_id`); the server relays to that one participant only and rewrites `to` → `from`. Unaddressed broadcast corrupts the receiver's single `RTCPeerConnection` for the sender
- `peer-joined` is broadcast from the SSE handler *after* the joiner subscribes, never from `handle_join`: a `broadcast` with no subscriber is dropped, and a lost offer strands that pair forever (the tie-break allows only one side to offer)
- SSE event types: `peer-joined`, `peer-left`, `offer`, `answer`, `ice-candidate`, `chat-message`
- One SSE stream per room visit, owned by `RoomPage` and closed in `on_cleanup`: dropping an `EventSource` does not close its connection, so an unowned stream leaks a socket per visit against the browser's 6-connections-per-host cap
- All signaling payloads are JSON (serde)

## Chat
- Chat messages sent via `POST /api/room/:id/chat?session_id=...`
- Chat history fetched via `GET /api/room/:id/chat/history`
- Chat broadcast to room members via SSE (`chat-message` event)
- Storage behind a `ChatStore` trait — currently `InMemoryChat` (per-room ring buffer, 200 msgs max)
- Future: swap to SQLite/Postgres by implementing `ChatStore` trait
- Messages are persisted immediately (not fire-and-forget) — append to store BEFORE broadcasting

## User Identity (optional)
- No accounts required for MVP
- Optional display name: entered by user, stored in browser cookie `wr_name` (1yr, SameSite=Lax)
- Stable user ID: auto-generated UUID v4, stored in cookie `wr_uid` (1yr, SameSite=Lax)
- Both sent in `JoinRequest.display_name` and `JoinRequest.user_id`
- Participant slot (`JoinRequest.session_id`, echoed back as `self_id`): UUID v4 in `sessionStorage` key `wr_sid` — deliberately NOT a cookie, so each tab is its own participant and a reload reuses the same slot
- Server stores display names per-room (in-memory DashMap)
- Cookie-based identity gives: consistent name across visits, trackable ID for moderation
- Future: cookie ID becomes the `sub` claim in a JWT when auth is added

## WebRTC
- `RTCPeerConnection` via `web-sys` (no JS glue files unless absolutely necessary)
- STUN: always configured (coturn or Google public for dev)
- TURN: ephemeral HMAC credentials served from `GET /api/turn-credentials`
- Media: audio + video (camera) by default, screen share as toggle
- Codec: prefer VP8, fallback to H264 (browser handles negotiation)
- Data channels: enabled for future collaborative features (drawing, reactions)
- No server-side media processing (no SFU for MVP — mesh up to ~4 peers)

## Server
- Framework: Axum 0.8 (paired with Leptos 0.8 fullstack)
- Async runtime: tokio
- State: `Arc<DashMap<String, Room>>` — in-memory, no DB
- SSE: `axum::response::Sse` + `tokio_stream::wrappers::UnboundedReceiverStream`
- CORS: same-origin only (no cross-origin for MVP)
- Graceful shutdown: `tokio::signal::ctrl_c` + `axum::serve` with graceful handle

## Build & Deploy
- Build: `cargo leptos build` (or `cargo leptos watch` in dev) builds wasm client + server in one pass
- Single crate: `lib` target → wasm (`--features=csr`), `webrtc-room` bin target → native (`--features=ssr`); configured in `[package.metadata.leptos]`
- No Trunk and no committed `index.html` — the server writes the HTML shell into `site-root` at startup
- `wasm-bindgen` crate is pinned with `=` and `wasm-bindgen-cli` must be installed at that exact version
- Single binary deploys: server binary + static files in `target/site/`
- coturn: separate process (not embedded)
- Reverse proxy: Caddy or Nginx for TLS (required — WebRTC mandates secure context)
- Docker: multi-stage Dockerfile for the server (`rust:…-slim` builder → `debian:bookworm-slim` runtime), coturn via `network_mode: host`
- Build tools (`cargo-leptos`, `wasm-bindgen-cli`, Dart Sass) are pinned in `scripts/ci-tools.sh` — the single source of truth, used by both CI and the Dockerfile. They are downloaded as prebuilt release binaries (musl statics for the two Rust tools), never `cargo install`-ed: unpinned resolution pulls a dependency tree whose MSRV outruns our toolchain, and it compiles ~300 crates nothing else needs
- `rust-toolchain.toml` is `channel = "stable"`, so the compiler moves with no commit attached to it; the Dockerfile's `rust:1.90-slim` is a bootstrap floor, not a pin. Pin the channel if reproducible builds ever start to matter

## File Naming
- Snake_case for modules (`room.rs`, `webrtc.rs`)
- PascalCase for components/types (`RoomPage`, `PeerState`)
- CSS classes: kebab-case only (`video-tile`, `room-controls`)

## Testing
- `#[cfg(test)]` for unit tests
- Integration tests hit real Axum server via `axum::test` helpers
- WebRTC logic: testable as pure Rust where possible (state machines, signaling message routing)
- Rust tests run in CI under `cargo nextest --profile ci` (`.config/nextest.toml`); `cargo test` cannot emit JUnit on stable because its JSON output is behind `-Z unstable-options`
- JS suites run on Node's built-in test runner (`node --test`) with two reporters at once — spec to stdout, JUnit to a file. Deliberately no npm project and no `package.json`
- Test titles stay constant across runs: per-run values (room names, ports, timestamps) end up in the JUnit `classname` and destroy per-test history in GitHub's checks. Log such values instead

## CI & GitHub Actions
- Runner is pinned to an explicit Ubuntu (`ubuntu-24.04`), never `ubuntu-latest` — that label is a moving target (it becomes Ubuntu 26.04 between 2026-10-19 and 2026-11-19). Move the pin on a branch, watch it go green, then land it
- `runs-on` may not use the `env` context (actionlint enforces this; available are `github`, `inputs`, `matrix`, `needs`, `strategy`, `vars`). `vars` would work but the runner OS does not belong in repository settings, where a fork receives an empty string and every job fails — keep the four literals
- Every suite publishes JUnit. `check_name` must be unique per job, because jobs on different runners overwrite each other's history; publishing requires `permissions.checks: write`; nextest writes `target/nextest/ci/junit.xml` regardless of `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` (its issue #883)
- CI tooling that is fetched rather than depended upon (`cargo-nextest`, and locally `actionlint`/`shellcheck`) never enters `Cargo.toml`
- Only `ci.yml` exists; there is no lint job. `actionlint`, `shellcheck` and `node --check` are run by hand before pushing a workflow or shell change — they are not gates, so a broken workflow reaches `main` if nobody remembers to run them
- The browser suite is advisory (`continue-on-error`): the job can be green while its check is red, so read the JUnit check rather than trusting the job tick
- A CI change is verified by a run, not by a linter. `actionlint` validates expressions and context availability, but only a runner proves an action version or a runner label
- Comments that state CI cost must match measured timing. `Build image` is ~3 min across runs precisely because the build tools are downloaded rather than compiled — an older comment claimed the opposite and misled the reader into distrusting a green run
- Watching CI over REST: `/actions/runs/{n}/jobs` takes the `run_id`, not the `run_number` (the latter returns 404, not an empty list); job **logs** are 403 without admin, so infer from step conclusions and timestamps; the `JUnit — …` entries returned by the jobs endpoint are check runs, not jobs (`labels` empty, `steps: 0`)

## Security
- TURN credentials: ephemeral (HMAC-SHA1 with TTL per TURN REST API spec — this is a protocol constraint, not a weakness; HMAC security is independent of hash collision resistance)
- Internal hashing (user IDs, future tokens): SHA-256 (sha2 crate)
- Password comparison: `constant_time_eq` crate (not `==`)
- SSE connection: authenticated on connect, re-validated on each signal POST
- No secrets in client bundle (TURN secret stays server-side)
- Input validation: room name regex `[a-z0-9-]{3,48}`, password max 128 chars, display name max 64 chars, chat message max 2000 chars
- Cookies: `SameSite=Lax`, `Path=/`, 1-year expiry — no `Secure` in dev (add via reverse proxy)
